.TH CCOL_SCOPED_PTR_RELEASE 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_scoped_ptr_release \- release ownership of a scoped pointer, preventing its auto-free
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <common.h>

released = ccol_scoped_ptr_release(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns name's current value and sets name to NULL, so the pending
cleanup registered by ccol_scoped_ptr() or ccol_scoped_ptr_mp() becomes
a no-op at scope exit. Use this to hand ownership of the pointer out of
the enclosing scope (for example, as a function's return value) instead
of having it freed there.

The caller receiving the released value becomes responsible for freeing
it eventually, with whichever allocator it was originally allocated
with.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  A pointer previously declared with ccol_scoped_ptr() or
      ccol_scoped_ptr_mp()
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns name's value immediately prior to release (NULL if name was
never assigned).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Requires GCC or Clang (the same requirement ccol_scoped_ptr()/
ccol_scoped_ptr_mp() already have)

Safe to call on a scoped pointer that was never assigned; returns NULL
and leaves name at NULL.

Calling this more than once on the same name is safe but pointless
after the first call: name is already NULL, so every subsequent call
simply returns NULL again.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
char *build(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp) {
  ccol_scoped_ptr_mp(buf, char, mp);
  buf = _mem_alloc(mp, 128);
  if (!buf) return NULL;

  /* ... populate buf ... */

  return ccol_scoped_ptr_release(buf);  /* caller now owns it */
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr (3),
.BR ccol_scoped_ptr_mp (3)
